  1160,Internal Language of Topoi in Algebraic Geometry,"https://personalpages.manchester.ac.uk/staff/Nicola.Gambino/pssl-101/Blechschmidt.pdf",ac.uk,math.AG,math.AG math.CT math.LO mathematics topos-theory algebraic-geometry schemes sheaf-theory algebraic-groups slides,null,null,null,"Internal Language of Topoi in Algebraic Geometry. Ingo Blechschmidt's slides on the petit and gros Zariski topoi: - **Petit Zariski Topos $\\operatorname{Shv}(X)$:** The structure sheaf $\\mathcal{O}_X$ internally behaves as a field (or local ring where every non-invertible element is nilpotent). A sheaf of modules $\\mathcal{F}$ is locally of finite type if and only if it is internally finitely generated. Coherent sheaves correspond internally to finitely presented modules. - **Gros Zariski Topos $\\operatorname{Zar}(S)$:** Represents schemes by their functors of points $\\mathbf{Sch}/S \\to \\mathbf{Set}$. The affine line $\\mathbb{A}^1$ behaves internally as a commutative field where every function $\\mathbb{A}^1 \\to \\mathbb{A}^1$ is a polynomial. Group schemes $\\mathbb{G}_a, \\mathbb{G}_m, \\mathrm{GL}_n$ appear internally as standard algebraic groups."
